questions.question:
What is the work function of the metal if the light of wavelength 4000$\mathop A\limits^ \circ$ generates photoelectrons of velocity 6 $\times 10^{5} ms^{–1}$ from it ?

(Mass of electron = 9 $\times 10^{–31}$ kg;

Velocity of light = 3 $\times 10^{8}$ ms^{ - 1}

Plank's constant = 6.626 $\times 10^{–34}$ Js;

Charge of electron = 1.6 $\times 10^{–19} JeV^{–1}$)

questions.explanation:
Energy of EMR = IE of the metal (A)

$= hv = {{hc} \over \lambda }$ atom^{ - 1} $= {{hc} \over \lambda } \times {N_A}$ mol^{ - 1}

$= {{(6.63 \times {{10}^{ - 34}}) \times (3 \times {{10}^8}) \times (6.02 \times {{10}^{23}})} \over {(663 \times {{10}^{ - 9}})}}$ J mol^{ - 1} [$\because \lambda$ = 663 nm = 663 $\times$ 10^{ - 9} m]

= 180600 J mol^{ - 1} = 180.6 kJ mol^{ - 1} $\simeq$ 181 kJ mol^{$-$1}

$\upsilon$ : speed of electron having max. K.E.

$\Rightarrow$ from Einstein equation : E = $\phi$ + K.E._{max}

$\Rightarrow {{hc} \over \lambda } = h{\upsilon _0} + \frac{1}{2}m{v^2} \Rightarrow {{6.63 \times {{10}^{ - 34}} \times 3 \times {{10}^8}} \over {500 \times {{10}^{ - 9}}}} = 6.63 \times {10^{ - 34}} \times 4.3 \times {10^{14}} + \frac{1}{2}m{v^2} \Rightarrow {{6.63 \times 30 \times {{10}^{ - 20}}} \over 5} = 6.63 \times 4.3 \times {10^{ - 20}} + \frac{1}{2}m{v^2} \Rightarrow 11.271 \times {10^{ - 20}}J = \frac{1}{2} \times 9 \times {10^{ - 31}} \times {\upsilon ^2} \Rightarrow \upsilon$ = 5 $\times 10^{5}$ m/sec.

$\lambda=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{\mathrm{mv}} $\begin{aligned} &\Rightarrow \mathrm{mv}=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{\lambda}=\frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \mathrm{~kg} \frac{\mathrm{m}^{2}}{\mathrm{sec}^{2}} \times \mathrm{sec}}{3.3 \times 10^{-10} \mathrm{~m}} \\\\ &\mathrm{mv}=\frac{6.626 \times 10^{-24}}{3.3}=2 \times 10^{-24} \mathrm{~kg} \mathrm{~m} \,\mathrm{sec}{ }^{-1} \end{aligned} 



Kinetic energy $=\frac{1}{2} m v^{2} $\begin{aligned} &=\frac{(\mathrm{mv})^{2}}{2 \mathrm{~m}} \\\\ &=$\frac{\left(2 \times 10^{-24}\right)$^{2}}{2 \times 9.1 \times 10^{-31} \mathrm{~kg}} \\\\ &=2.18 \times 10^{-18} \mathrm{~J} \\\\ &=21.8 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{~J} \end{aligned} 



Total energy absorbed  =  lonization energy $+$ Kinetic energy$$\begin{aligned} &=(21.76+21.8) \times 10^{-19} \\\\ &=43.56 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{~J} \\\\ &\approx 2 \text { times of } 21.76 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{~J} \end{aligned}$$
